# Retire FBX production ingestion and build native assets from normalized JSON

Replace FBX as the production Unreal mesh and scene boundary with deterministic
native asset construction from normalized extracted JSON and versioned plans.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Build Static Mesh, Skeletal Mesh, Skeleton, animation, Physics Asset, and
  related native assets directly from normalized source evidence.
- [ ] Apply source-to-Unreal basis and unit conversion exactly once in typed
  native construction code, never through FBX export-root compensation.
- [ ] Prove native read-back parity for representative world, prop, character,
  interior, and vehicle assets before removing each FBX consumer.
- [ ] Keep the FBX writer available only as deprecated interoperability and
  diagnostic tooling until no production Unreal plan depends on it.
- [ ] Remove obsolete FBX-specific axis rotations, root reflections, scene-unit
  workarounds, and material-slot transport policy after native parity exists.
- [ ] Regenerate the complete project from normalized evidence without requiring
  an FBX file for production Unreal asset construction.

## Current retirement order

- [ ] Replace vehicle FBX publication in
  `vehicle_catalog/prepare.rs` and the
  `vehicle-skeletal-mesh-fbx-v1` plan step with a native Skeletal Mesh and
  Skeleton constructor fed by normalized geometry, rig, and skin evidence.
- [ ] Decouple `vehicle_physics_prerequisites.py`,
  `vehicle_physics_construction.py`, and `vehicle_material_slot_binding.py` from
  the deprecated FBX route and join them to stable native mesh identity.
- [ ] Replace world and prop FBX publication, including `ReflectX` in
  `prop_catalog/world_level/export.rs`, with one typed native basis conversion.
- [ ] Replace character and generic static/skeletal FBX routes after equivalent
  native geometry, rig, skin, animation, and material constructors have parity.
- [ ] Remove production FBX catalogs, staging prerequisites, and generic Unreal
  FBX execution routes only after no production plan references them.

## 2026-09-12 production-seam audit

The current `vehicle.catalog.json` retains an FBX artifact summary but not the
complete vertex, triangle, normal, UV, skin-weight, and bind-pose payload needed
for direct Skeletal Mesh construction. That data exists transiently during
vehicle preparation through model types currently housed under `formats/fbx`.

The first implementation checkpoint is therefore engine-neutral model evidence,
not another Unreal importer. Move the reusable geometry, rig, skin, and
animation
representation out of FBX ownership and publish a versioned normalized JSON
payload before implementing the native Unreal constructor.

`SharImportEditor` currently has no production direct-mesh builder based on
`MeshDescription` or equivalent skeletal construction data. Add that capability
only after the normalized model payload has a deterministic schema, provenance,
hashes, and representative vehicle fixtures.

## 2026-09-12 normalized vehicle model checkpoint

Vehicle preparation now publishes `model.normalized.json` before the deprecated
FBX target-basis rebase. Schema `shar.normalized-skeletal-model.v1` retains the
complete render mesh groups, source basis, skeleton hierarchy and rest matrices,
skin influences, source rig metadata, and package-local animation samples.

Two fresh full-corpus exports produced 88 standalone vehicle models from the 89
car P3Ds, excluding only the shared `common` dependency package. All 88 model
payloads and the root catalog were byte-identical between runs. Snake wheel
bones remained in source `+X right, +Y up, +Z forward` coordinates, proving the
Chaos `(Z,X,Y)` conversion did not leak into normalized evidence.

`prepare-unreal` now verifies each normalized model path, bytes, SHA-256,
schema,
identity, source coordinate contract, array counts, and the explicit absence of
target-basis conversion. FBX remains required only by the current compatibility
import route. The next implementation checkpoint is the direct native Skeletal
Mesh and Skeleton constructor consuming this verified model payload.

## 2026-09-12 native vehicle decode checkpoint

`SharImportEditor` now owns a native normalized-vehicle decode kernel for
`shar.normalized-skeletal-model.v1`. It validates the declared source basis,
units, hierarchy, topology, vertex channels, skin identities, and the same
per-vertex weight invariant used by the normalized Rust domain. It rejects
already target-rebased payloads before UObject construction.

The kernel applies the vehicle source-to-Unreal conversion exactly once:
positions use `(Z, X, Y) * 100` from source meters to Unreal centimeters,
directions use `(Z, X, Y)`, and local rest matrices use the corresponding
proper basis conjugation with translation scaled once. Synthetic asymmetric
automation tests cover the mapping and fail-closed cases.

A headless Unreal corpus probe decoded all 88 standalone normalized vehicle
models with unique model identities. A Snake probe additionally verified the
29 retained bones, 39 render parts, one animation clip, and asymmetric `w0`
rest translation after conversion. No FBX import was used for these probes.

This checkpoint does not yet publish `USkeletalMesh` or `USkeleton`. The
production vehicle route therefore remains the deprecated FBX compatibility
route until native object construction and read-back parity are implemented.

## 2026-09-12 native skeletal UObject checkpoint

`SharImportEditor` can now construct transient `USkeleton` and `USkeletalMesh`
objects directly from the validated vehicle recipe. It materializes the decoded
reference skeleton, target-basis rest transforms, and authored material-slot
identity without passing through FBX.

The same builder now creates LOD 0 source `MeshDescription` data and commits the
normalized positions, normals, UV0, vertex colors, triangles, polygon groups,
bones, and default skin weights. A synthetic asymmetric automation test reached
`Result={Success}` after the required source-model, LOD-info, and imported-model
LOD records were initialized through Unreal's native skeletal mesh API.

This is still a transient construction checkpoint. Render-data build,
persisted package publication, and persisted read-back parity remain before the
production vehicle route can stop using the deprecated FBX compatibility path.

## Dependencies

The normalized extraction and provenance ledgers remain source authority. Native
construction must consume those verified records rather than reparsing raw game
packages inside Unreal.

## Native Unreal asset pivot — 2026-09-12

Normalized extracted JSON and versioned normalized plans are the canonical
production handoff into Unreal. FBX is deprecated for production Unreal asset
construction and may remain only as temporary interoperability, inspection, or
migration evidence. New implementation work must construct target assets through
tested native Unreal automation without making FBX transforms, scene units,
material slots, or importer behavior authoritative. Existing FBX-based evidence
remains historical or transitional and must be retired from production once the
equivalent native constructor has parity coverage.

## Evidence

The vehicle work demonstrated why the boundary must move. Correct vehicle
geometry required compensating for FBX export-root, importer-axis, scene-unit,
and root-local Chaos semantics across multiple layers even though normalized P3D
evidence already contained the source geometry, skeleton, animation, collision,
and material records.

The accepted native Physics Asset and material toolsets show the replacement
pattern. They consume typed plans, construct Unreal objects directly, verify
read-back, preserve stable object identity, and fail closed without asking FBX
to encode target-specific runtime policy.
